     1	# Branch protection + integrity policy
     2	
     3	> This document replaces the PLAN-001 P2 proposal for cryptographic
     4	> skill signing. The decision record is `.claude/adr/ADR-003-branch-
     5	> protection-replaces-skill-signing.md`.
     6	
     7	## Why branch protection instead of crypto
     8	
     9	A SKILL.md hash file (`.claude/skills/INTEGRITY.sha256`) can be modified
    10	by the same actor who modifies the SKILL itself. Crypto doesn't defend
    11	against an authorized author making a weakening change — that's a
    12	**review** problem, not a **crypto** problem.
    13	
    14	Git commit hashes already provide cryptographic integrity. What's
    15	missing is a **review gate** that forces human attention on every
    16	change to protected paths. GitHub branch protection + CODEOWNERS is
    17	that gate.
    18	
    19	See `.claude/adr/ADR-003-branch-protection-replaces-skill-signing.md`
    20	for the full rationale.
    21	
    22	## When to enable
    23	
    24	**AFTER** Sprint 2 ships and CI is verified green. Per PLAN-002 §11-bis
    25	Q3, Sprint 2's 15–18 atomic commits push directly to `main` (same
    26	pattern as Sprint 1). The solo Owner approving their own PRs on every
    27	commit would add friction without buying security.
    28	
    29	Sprint 3 onwards operates fully under branch protection.
    30	
    31	## Setup: main branch rules
    32	
    33	Go to **Settings → Branches → Add rule** for pattern `main`:
    34	
    35	```
    36	[x] Require a pull request before merging
    37	    [x] Require approvals
    38	        Number of approvals required: 1
    39	    [x] Dismiss stale pull request approvals when new commits are pushed
    40	    [x] Require review from Code Owners
    41	[x] Require status checks to pass before merging
    42	    [x] Require branches to be up to date before merging
    43	    Select status checks:
    44	      - validate / Governance, health, contamination, shellcheck
    45	      (and "Skill benchmarks (advisory)" once the secret is configured)
    46	[x] Require conversation resolution before merging
    47	[x] Require signed commits                                 (optional)
    48	[x] Require linear history                                 (optional)
    49	[x] Do not allow bypassing the above settings
    50	[x] Restrict who can push to matching branches             (solo repo: skip)
    51	```
    52	
    53	Leave the following **unchecked** unless you have a specific reason:
    54	
    55	```
    56	[ ] Allow force pushes
    57	[ ] Allow deletions
    58	```
    59	
    60	## CODEOWNERS
    61	
    62	The `.github/CODEOWNERS` file is already in the repo (shipped in
    63	Sprint 2 C.3). It protects:
    64	
    65	```
    66	.claude/skills/**                       @<owner>
    67	.claude/skills/**/benchmarks/**         @<owner>
    68	.claude/hooks/**                        @<owner>
    69	.claude/plans/PLAN-*.md                 @<owner>
    70	.claude/adr/**                          @<owner>
    71	PROTOCOL.md                             @<owner>
    72	.claude/scripts/validate-governance.sh  @<owner>
    73	.claude/scripts/check-contamination.sh  @<owner>
    74	.github/workflows/validate.yml          @<owner>
    75	.claude/settings.json                   @<owner>
    76	templates/settings/**                   @<owner>
    77	```
    78	
    79	When you install this framework in a new project, **replace
    80	`@<owner>` with the actual GitHub handle** of the human who will
    81	review changes. The repo's live `.github/CODEOWNERS` already carries
    82	the real handle; the `@<owner>` above is the template placeholder.
    83	
    84	### Adding teammates
    85	
    86	When you have a second reviewer:
    87	
    88	1. Add their GitHub handle to the lines in `.github/CODEOWNERS` that
    89	   they should also gate
    90	2. In branch protection, bump "Number of approvals required" to 2 if
    91	   you want two-reviewer review
    92	3. Commit the CODEOWNERS change through a PR (which requires approval
    93	   from the existing CODEOWNER — correct behavior, no paradox)
    94	
    95	## What branch protection catches
    96	
    97	- Unreviewed changes to skills (the weakening-without-review threat
    98	  from ADR-003)
    99	- Unreviewed changes to the governance layer (hooks, validate-governance,
   100	  contamination check)
   101	- Unreviewed changes to plans and ADRs
   102	- Unreviewed changes to CI
   103	- Force pushes to main that would rewrite history
   104	- Direct pushes to main from someone who isn't configured
   105	
   106	## What branch protection does NOT catch
   107	
   108	- A Code Owner intentionally weakening a skill and approving their
   109	  own PR. (Solution: second reviewer, or pre-commit CI that catches
   110	  the specific weakening.)
   111	- A compromised Code Owner account. (Solution: 2FA on the account,
   112	  GitHub audit log review, key rotation.)
   113	- Changes landed via direct git push from an admin. (Solution: do
   114	  NOT check "Allow administrators to bypass" — leave the setting
   115	  off.)
   116	
   117	## API Key Hygiene (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY)
   118	
   119	The benchmarks workflow (`.github/workflows/benchmarks.yml`) uses
   120	`${{ secrets.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY }}` to call the Anthropic API during
   121	skill benchmarks. Per PLAN-002 §11-bis Q6:
   122	
   123	### Rotation policy
   124	
   125	- **When:** rotate on suspicion of compromise (log leak, fork PR
   126	  exfiltration attempt, Anthropic Console anomaly alert). **NOT** on
   127	  a calendar rotation — calendar rotation of a low-privilege
   128	  read-only API key adds human-error surface without proportional
   129	  benefit.
   130	- **Who:** Owner, manually.
   131	- **Rotation procedure:**
   132	  1. Generate a new key at Anthropic Console (console.anthropic.com)
   133	  2. Update the `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` secret at
   134	     **GitHub Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions**
   135	  3. Revoke the old key at the Anthropic Console
   136	  4. Record the rotation (date + reason, NOT the key) at
   137	     `docs/rotation-log.md`
   138	  5. Monitor the next CI run to confirm the new key works
   139	
   140	### Defense in depth
   141	
   142	- `run-skill-benchmark.py` imports `_lib.redact.redact_secrets()` and
   143	  runs every API response through it before writing
   144	  `benchmark-results.json`. If a prompt-inject scenario asks the model
   145	  to echo its own credentials, the echo is redacted before it lands
   146	  in a CI artifact.
   147	- The workflow refuses to run on fork PRs
   148	  (`github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository`
   149	  guard). `pull_request_target` is EXPLICITLY FORBIDDEN — it would let
   150	  a fork PR inject code that runs with the secret.
   151	- The workflow uses a narrow `paths:` filter so docs/hooks/plans PRs
   152	  never pay API cost (and never trigger the secret-gated job).
   153	
   154	### Scope
   155	
   156	The Anthropic API key used here should be a **project-scoped** key
   157	with read-only usage (no admin, no write). A compromise bounds the
   158	damage to "attacker burns some of your quota", mitigated by Anthropic
   159	Console's own spend alerts.
   160	
   161	## CI gates (Sprint 3 Item B)
   162	
   163	The framework ships with two distinct floor gates on benchmark scores:
   164	
   165	| Gate | Threshold | Source | Effect |
   166	|------|-----------|--------|--------|
   167	| **CRITICAL floor** | overall score < 0.4 | `scoring.health_thresholds.critical` in benchmark YAML | `run-skill-benchmark.py` exits 1. CI fails. Always enforced — no opt-out. |
   168	| **Absolute floor** | overall score < 0.6 | `--floor 0.6` CLI flag (passed by `benchmarks.yml`) | Same exit code (rc=1). CI fails. Can be tuned per-environment. |
   169	
   170	Both gates share exit code 1 by design (debate round 1 consensus
   171	R-DEV1): having separate exit codes for "CRITICAL" vs "absolute floor"
   172	fragments CI error handling without buying clarity. Distinguish via
   173	the `$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY` table, which `benchmarks.yml` assembles from the
   174	per-benchmark Markdown that `.claude/scripts/run-skill-benchmark.py` emits
   175	with `--markdown`. (The framework's own `.github/scripts/` is not part of an
   176	install — see the header of `.github/workflows/benchmarks.yml.template`.)
   177	
   178	### Escalation path
   179	
   180	- CRITICAL floor breach → the benchmark scenario scoring is broken, or
   181	  the skill has regressed catastrophically. Owner debugs the skill's
   182	  prompt or the scenario expectations.
   183	- Absolute floor breach (but above CRITICAL) → the skill has soft
   184	  regressed. Look at the step summary to see which scenarios failed.
   185	  For each failure, a **Reflexion lesson** is written to
   186	  `$HOME/.claude/projects/<slug>/lessons/` (Sprint 3 Item A) — next
   187	  spawn of a relevant agent will see that lesson under
   188	  `## PAST LESSONS`.
   189	
   190	### Sprint 4 planned
   191	
   192	- **Regression gate** — any drop vs `main` last-known-good fails CI.
   193	  Requires historical benchmark storage. Deferred per PLAN-002 §11-bis
   194	  Q6.
   195	
   196	## Quick checklist
   197	
   198	- [ ] `.github/CODEOWNERS` is in the repo (shipped Sprint 2 C.3)
   199	- [ ] Branch protection rule for `main` is configured per this doc
   200	- [ ] "Allow administrators to bypass" is **unchecked**
   201	- [ ] `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` secret is set under **Settings → Secrets**
   202	- [ ] `docs/rotation-log.md` exists (even if empty) so rotations have
   203	      a place to land
   204	- [ ] Absolute floor `0.6` is appropriate for the project (lower it if
   205	      scenarios are noisy; higher only after stability established)

